<DocsLayout
  title="API and CLI reference"
  description="SuiteCut fixture methods, capture settings, reporter options, and render flags."
  section="api"
>
  <h1>API and CLI</h1>
  <p>
    SuiteCut's fixture, capture, output, reporter, and render option objects reject unknown keys and
    invalid values. Playwright launch and browser-context options pass through to Playwright.
  </p>

  <h2 id="public-exports">Public exports</h2>
  <p>
    These are every package entry point and export in the current build. Files below
    <code>suitecut/src</code> are internal and are not public imports.
  </p>

  <h3><code>suitecut</code></h3>
  <p>The runner-free recorder, renderer, and their public option and result types.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={rootExports} />

  <h3><code>suitecut/test</code></h3>
  <p>The optional Playwright Test fixture and assertion API.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={testExports} />

  <h3><code>suitecut/playwright</code></h3>
  <p>
    A compatibility alias for the runner-free exports from <code>suitecut</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={playwrightExports} />

  <h3><code>suitecut/audio-plugin</code></h3>
  <p>The contract and helpers for external narration packages.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={audioPluginExports} />

  <h3><code>suitecut/render</code></h3>
  <p>The programmatic renderer and its complete request, output, edit, and report types.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={renderExports} />

  <h3><code>suitecut/reporter</code></h3>
  <p>The Playwright reporter class and reporter configuration type.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={reporterExports} />

  <h3><code>suitecut/types</code></h3>
  <p>The complete manifest, event, artifact, media, geometry, diagnostic, and identity model.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={typeExports} />

  <h2 id="playwright-test-api">Playwright Test</h2>
  <p>
    Import from <code>suitecut/test</code>. <code>test</code> extends Playwright Test with the
    <code>suitecut</code> fixture and two options. <code>expect</code> is Playwright's normal assertion
    API.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>suitecut</code>: the recording controls documented below.</li>
    <li>
      <code>suitecutCapture</code>: viewport, source size, frame rate, quality, and narration tail.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>suitecutAudioPlugins</code>: external provider references loaded in the narration
      worker.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="fixture-methods">Fixture methods</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={fixtureInterface} />

  <h3><code>selectPage(page): void</code></h3>
  <p>
    Selects the main page, popup, or secondary page used by later narration, checkpoints, holds, and
    <code>scrollTop()</code>. Locator methods select their locator's page automatically. Closing a
    selected popup returns selection to its opener when possible.
  </p>

  <h3><code>narrate(text, options?): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Synthesizes speech locally, records its audio, displays a caption, and waits for the measured
    clip plus the configured narration tail. Synthesis time is excluded from the recording clock.
    The
    <code>text</code> must not be empty.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>provider</code>: <code>kokoro</code> by default, <code>macos-say</code>, or a configured
      audio plugin ID.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>voice</code>: <code>af_heart</code> for Kokoro; the native provider uses its system default
      when omitted.
    </li>
    <li><code>speed</code>: <code>0.5</code> through <code>2</code>; default <code>1</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>caption</code>: override the spoken text shown on screen. Use an empty string to hide
      the caption without removing audio.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3><code>checkpoint(label, options?): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Marks the current page state and attaches a WebM clip cut from the same page screencast used by
    the final render. The label must not be empty. <code>durationMs</code> sets the recording window;
    the default is <code>500</code> milliseconds and the maximum is <code>10000</code>.
  </p>

  <h3><code>hold(durationMs): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Keeps the selected browser page recording its current state. The duration is in milliseconds and
    must be greater than zero.
  </p>

  <h3><code>highlight(locator, options?): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Measures a visible locator and records the complete highlight animation in the selected browser.
    Hidden or geometry-less locators fail clearly.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>durationMs</code>: total duration; default <code>1200</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>mode</code>: <code>outline</code> by default, <code>fill</code>, or <code
        >spotlight</code
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>geometry</code>: <code>element</code> by default, or <code>content</code> to tightly wrap
      rendered text and controls inside a block.
    </li>
    <li><code>paddingPx</code>: <code>8</code>; <code>borderWidthPx</code>: <code>4</code>.</li>
    <li><code>borderStyle</code>: <code>solid</code> by default, or <code>dashed</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>borderColor</code>: <code>#7C3AED</code>; <code>borderRadiusPx</code>: <code>10</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>fillColor</code>: <code>#7C3AED</code>; <code>fillOpacity</code>: <code>0.08</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>backdropColor</code>: <code>#000000</code>; <code>backdropOpacity</code>: <code
        >0.45</code
      >.
    </li>
    <li><code>label</code>: optional text displayed above the target.</li>
    <li>
      <code>enter</code>: default <code
        >{`{ type: 'fade', durationMs: 180, easing: 'ease-out' }`}</code
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>exit</code>: default <code
        >{`{ type: 'fade', durationMs: 140, easing: 'ease-in' }`}</code
      >.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Animation type accepts <code>none</code>, <code>fade</code>, <code>scale</code>, or
    <code>fade-scale</code>. Easing accepts <code>linear</code>, <code>ease-in</code>,
    <code>ease-out</code>, or <code>ease-in-out</code>. A <code>none</code> phase is instantaneous. Scale
    emphasis never transforms the highlight box, so its border stays aligned throughout the phase.
  </p>

  <h3><code>zoom(locator, options?): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Scrolls the locator into view and records a continuously evaluated camera move. SuiteCut centers
    edge targets, pads beyond the captured browser frame when needed, and limits the requested scale
    when necessary to retain available target padding. Concurrent zoom calls run in call order.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>scale</code>: <code>1</code> through <code>1.25</code>; default <code>1.15</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>geometry</code>: <code>element</code> by default, or <code>content</code> to center rendered
      text and controls inside a block.
    </li>
    <li><code>paddingPx</code>: minimum retained target padding; default <code>24</code>.</li>
    <li><code>holdMs</code>: time at the closest scale; default <code>900</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>enter</code>: default <code
        >{`{ type: 'scale', durationMs: 260, easing: 'ease-out' }`}</code
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>exit</code>: default <code
        >{`{ type: 'scale', durationMs: 220, easing: 'ease-in-out' }`}</code
      >.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Zoom accepts the same animation values as highlight. <code>none</code> disables a phase;
    <code>fade</code>, <code>scale</code>, and <code>fade-scale</code> use eased camera interpolation.
  </p>

  <h3><code>hover(locator, options?): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Scrolls the locator into view, moves the recorded cursor to its center, performs a real
    Playwright hover, waits for resulting application animations by default, and settles.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>moveDurationMs</code>: <code>450</code>.</li>
    <li><code>settleMs</code>: <code>120</code>.</li>
    <li><code>waitForAnimations</code>: <code>true</code>.</li>
    <li><code>animationTimeoutMs</code>: <code>2000</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3><code>click(locator, options?): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Uses the same options as <code>hover()</code>, performs a real click, and records a 280 ms
    ripple. The ripple and optional application-animation wait run together. Click-driven navigation
    is supported.
  </p>

  <h3><code>type(locator, text, options?): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Scrolls the field into view and enters text one character at a time through Playwright's
    keyboard events. The source recording shows each character as it appears. The text must contain
    at least one character.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>delayMs</code>: time between characters; default <code>70</code>.</li>
    <li><code>settleMs</code>: wait after the last character; default <code>250</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>clearExisting</code>: replace the current value before typing; default <code>true</code
      >. Set it to <code>false</code> to type at the current caret.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3><code>scrollTo(locator, options?): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Calls native <code>scrollIntoView()</code> and waits for the target to remain stable for three animation
    frames.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>behavior</code>: <code>smooth</code> by default, or <code>auto</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>block</code>: <code>center</code> by default; also <code>start</code>, <code>end</code>,
      or <code>nearest</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>inline</code>: <code>nearest</code> by default; also <code>start</code>, <code
        >center</code
      >, or <code>end</code>.
    </li>
    <li><code>timeoutMs</code>: <code>2000</code>; <code>settleMs</code>: <code>120</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3><code>scrollTop(options?): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code></h3>
  <p>
    Scrolls the selected window to the origin. It uses <code>behavior</code>, <code>timeoutMs</code
    >, and
    <code>settleMs</code> with the defaults above. <code>block</code> and <code>inline</code> are accepted
    for the shared options type but do not affect a window-to-origin scroll.
  </p>

  <h2 id="capture-options">Capture options</h2>
  <p>
    Set <code>suitecutCapture</code> through <code>test.use()</code> or Playwright Test configuration.
    Plain Playwright scripts pass the same object as <code>capture</code> to <code>record()</code> or
    <code>defineSuiteCut()</code>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>viewport</code>: default <code>1600x900</code>; maximum <code>7680x4320</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>size</code>: encoded source size, defaulting to the viewport. A larger proportional size
      uses a matching layout scale. For example, a <code>1920x1080</code> viewport with a
      <code>3840x2160</code> size keeps HD composition on a native 4K surface. Both size dimensions must
      be even and no larger than <code>7680x4320</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>framesPerSecond</code>: <code>30</code> or <code>60</code>; default <code>30</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>quality</code>: screencast JPEG quality <code>1</code> through <code>100</code>; default <code
        >100</code
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>narrationTailMs</code>: non-negative post-narration pause; default <code>350</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Set <code>capture.stream</code> to publish H.264 video with silent AAC audio over RTMP or RTMPS. This
    selects streaming-only mode, with no source recordings or checkpoint clips. See <a
      href={sitePath('/docs/streaming')}>live streaming</a
    > for options, reconnect behavior, cancellation, and supported effects. Streaming JPEG quality defaults
    to <code>90</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reporter-options">Reporter options</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>outputFile</code>: default <code>.suitecut/latest-run.json</code>. The
      <code>SUITECUT_MANIFEST_PATH</code> environment variable takes precedence.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>pathKind</code>: <code>absolute</code> by default, or <code>manifest-relative</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>includeStepCategories</code>: optional allowlist of <code>hook</code>, <code
        >fixture</code
      >,
      <code>pw:api</code>, <code>expect</code>, <code>test.step</code>, <code>test.attach</code>, or
      <code>unknown</code>. Retained descendants reconnect to the nearest retained ancestor.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="recording-options">Recording options</h2>
  <p>
    Import <code>record</code> or <code>defineSuiteCut</code> from <code>suitecut</code>. Each call
    owns one browser, context, main page, and recording. It writes one manifest attempt with no test
    steps.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={plainPlaywrightSignatures} />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>browserName</code>: <code>chromium</code>, <code>firefox</code>, or <code>webkit</code>.
    </li>
    <li><code>launch</code>: Playwright launch options.</li>
    <li>
      <code>context</code>: Playwright browser context options. Do not set <code>recordVideo</code>;
      SuiteCut owns the page screencasts.
    </li>
    <li><code>capture</code>: the capture options listed above.</li>
    <li>
      <code>output.directory</code>: artifact directory. The default is a unique directory under
      <code>.suitecut/recordings</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>output.manifestPath</code>: default <code>.suitecut/latest-run.json</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>output.pathKind</code>: <code>absolute</code> by default, or <code
        >manifest-relative</code
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>signal</code>: an <code>AbortSignal</code> that cancels setup, capture, narration, and owned
      browser resources. Setup and recording callbacks receive the same signal.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3><code>SuiteCutRecordResult</code></h3>
  <p>
    A successful recording returns <code>attemptId</code>, <code>testId</code>, the decoded
    <code>manifest</code>, its <code>manifestPath</code>, and the <code>outputDirectory</code>.
    <code>defineSuiteCut()</code> can add typed setup values and reverse-order cleanup callbacks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="render-api">Renderer</h2>
  <p>
    <code>renderSuiteCut(request)</code> selects one recorded attempt, invokes FFmpeg, writes the requested
    video, and writes <code>&lt;output&gt;.suitecut.json</code>. It returns the same render report.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={renderExample} />
  <p>
    The request accepts <code>manifestPath</code>, <code>outputPath</code>, optional test and retry
    selection, optional render configuration, and an optional <code>signal</code>. Aborting stops
    the owned FFmpeg process and rejects with the signal reason. The report includes timing edits,
    diagnostics, selected IDs, output status, and the FFmpeg command result.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audio-plugin-api">Audio plugin helpers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>defineSuiteCutAudioPlugin(plugin)</code> preserves the plugin type without wrapping the runtime
      object.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>audioPluginReference(reference)</code> validates a provider ID, module name, and optional
      JSON settings before the worker loads them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>encodePcm16Wav(samples, sampleRate)</code> converts a mono <code>Float32Array</code> into
      a 16-bit PCM WAV byte array.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>SuiteCutAudioPlugin.synthesize(request)</code> receives text, voice, speed, output path, and
      plugin settings. It resolves after writing the WAV file.
    </li>
    <li>
      Optional <code>SuiteCutAudioPlugin.dispose()</code> releases plugin-owned models, workers, files,
      or subprocesses when the narration worker closes normally.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="../audio-plugins">audio plugin guide</a> shows package installation and provider setup.
  </p>

  <h2 id="command-line-flags">Command-line flags</h2>
  <p>
    <code>suitecut test [--manifest path] -- [Playwright options]</code> forwards Playwright arguments
    and installs the SuiteCut reporter. <code>suitecut render</code> requires
    <code>--manifest &lt;path&gt;</code> and <code>--output &lt;path&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--test-id &lt;id&gt;</code>: select a test; default is the first.</li>
    <li><code>--retry &lt;number&gt;</code>: select an attempt; default is the latest.</li>
    <li>
      <code>--container &lt;mp4|webm|mov|mkv&gt;</code>: default comes from the output extension.
    </li>
    <li><code>--format</code>: deprecated alias for <code>--container</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>--video-codec</code>: default <code>libvpx-vp9</code> for WebM, <code>libx264</code> otherwise.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>--audio-codec</code>: default <code>libopus</code> for WebM, <code>aac</code> otherwise.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>--pixel-format</code>: default <code>yuv422p10le</code> for ProRes, <code>yuv420p</code> otherwise.
    </li>
    <li><code>--color-range &lt;auto|full|limited&gt;</code>: default <code>auto</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>--width</code> and <code>--height</code>: default <code>1920x1080</code>; supply both,
      even, maximum <code>7680x4320</code>.
    </li>
    <li><code>--fps &lt;30|60&gt;</code>: default <code>30</code>.</li>
    <li><code>--quality &lt;standard|high|master&gt;</code>: default <code>standard</code>.</li>
    <li><code>--result-hold-ms</code>: non-negative final-frame hold; default <code>0</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>--background-color</code>: default <code>#0B1020</code>; three- or six-digit hex or the
      basic names black, white, red, green, blue, and yellow.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>--failure-mode &lt;strict|best-effort&gt;</code>: default <code>strict</code>.
      Best-effort can omit unavailable narration and records an <code>OPTIONAL_TRACK_OMITTED</code> warning;
      required video failures remain fatal.
    </li>
    <li><code>--ffmpeg-path</code>: explicit FFmpeg executable path.</li>
    <li><code>--no-narration</code>: omit narration audio; recorded captions remain.</li>
  </ul>

